WEBVTT


00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.500
Downloaded @ subs4free.club


00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:47.966
<i>He was a god.</i>


00:02:48.049 --> 00:02:49.551
He couldn't be any better.


00:02:53.555 --> 00:02:55.808
<i>When you're a kid, you need an idol,</i>


00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:57.725
<i>you need someone to inspire you.</i>


00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:01.771
And Fangio has inspired millions of people


00:03:01.854 --> 00:03:03.941
who wanted to become racers.


00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:22.668
<i>You just had to watch him driving.
It was as if he drove in slow motion</i>


00:03:22.751 --> 00:03:28.592
because of his class,
his intelligence, and his focus.


00:03:28.674 --> 00:03:29.925
He didn't make mistakes.


00:04:03.755 --> 00:04:10.428
I think... my life is divided
into several phases.


00:04:10.512 --> 00:04:14.058
Since I was a boy in school,
I wanted to be the best.


00:04:18.062 --> 00:04:21.273
<i>My father wanted me to work with him,</i>


00:04:21.356 --> 00:04:24.235
<i>but I told him,
"I want to learn mechanics."</i>


00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:30.616
<i>Nobody would teach you back then,</i>


00:04:30.699 --> 00:04:34.453
<i>you had to learn on your own
watching others.</i>


00:04:35.621 --> 00:04:39.793
<i>That's why for my first race,
I was the co-driver.</i>


00:04:39.875 --> 00:04:42.672
<i>I was... 18 years old.</i>


00:04:42.754 --> 00:04:47.093
<i>I participated in that race
without my father's permission, even.</i>


00:04:47.176 --> 00:04:49.969
<i>And I felt that the car was alive!</i>


00:04:52.472 --> 00:04:58.313
<i>Little by little,
he stepped into his passion...</i>


00:04:58.396 --> 00:04:59.980
car racing.


00:05:00.065 --> 00:05:05.403
And then in the 40s, something new
called road racing came about.


00:05:06.238 --> 00:05:12.703
<i>Since we had no assistance on the road
and there were few repair shops around...</i>


00:05:12.785 --> 00:05:14.412
<i>there were almost no repair shops,</i>


00:05:14.495 --> 00:05:20.712
<i>so we would carry spare parts inside
the car in case something broke down,</i>


00:05:20.794 --> 00:05:27.260
<i>and gasoline. We'd take
250 liters of gas with us in the car.</i>


00:05:28.094 --> 00:05:32.765
The car weighed
about 1,700 or 1,800 kilos,


00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:37.186
so it needed some heavy reinforcement.


00:05:37.271 --> 00:05:43.277
In these kinds of races, out of 150 cars
that crossed the starting line,


00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:47.196
around 20 cars would make it to the end.


00:05:49.158 --> 00:05:52.327
<i>He won the championships
of 1940 and 1941,</i>


00:05:52.411 --> 00:05:56.707
<i>but what made him popular
was the Buenos Aires-Lima race.</i>


00:05:56.790 --> 00:06:02.713
It was a long-distance race
where they drove for days,


00:06:02.796 --> 00:06:07.677
and it was very representative
of those days' road racing.


00:06:25.864 --> 00:06:30.619
<i>During World War II,</i>


00:06:30.787 --> 00:06:36.334
<i>I had to scrape out an existence.</i>


00:06:36.417 --> 00:06:39.045
<i>Tires were scarce back then,</i>


00:06:39.127 --> 00:06:44.759
<i>so I would buy trucks for their tires,
keep the trucks and sell the tires.</i>


00:06:45.593 --> 00:06:50.933
<i>I traveled Argentina's south provinces,
the entire Patagonia,</i>


00:06:51.015 --> 00:06:54.727
<i>buying trucks... just for their tires!</i>


00:06:55.562 --> 00:07:00.318
When the war was over, I went back
to those places to sell trucks,


00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:02.611
because I knew they didn't have any.


00:07:02.695 --> 00:07:07.282
<i>And I returned to the races in 47.</i>


00:07:07.367 --> 00:07:12.705
<i>During that time,
we had 4 different races in Argentina,</i>


00:07:12.788 --> 00:07:16.209
<i>and due to our good performance,</i>


00:07:16.291 --> 00:07:21.090
<i>the Argentine Automobile Club awarded
Galvez, Puopolo and me</i>


00:07:21.172 --> 00:07:24.175
<i>a trip to Europe.</i>


00:07:26.886 --> 00:07:31.267
GALLIATE, ITALY


00:07:34.729 --> 00:07:37.565
<i>When I raced in Europe,</i>


00:07:37.648 --> 00:07:43.654
<i>my center was a town called Galliate,
in the Novara province,</i>


00:07:43.738 --> 00:07:45.531
<i>30 kilometers from Milan.</i>


00:07:45.615 --> 00:07:50.453
<i>Mr. Varzi let me live
in his house for free.</i>


00:07:50.536 --> 00:07:54.791
<i>Varzi was already famous
in Argentina since 1947 or 1948,</i>


00:07:54.876 --> 00:07:59.838
and he invited the entire team
to visit his town, Galliate,


00:07:59.921 --> 00:08:04.385
and to stay in his house in Galliate,
where he wanted to establish


00:08:04.468 --> 00:08:07.763
the team's base of operations.


00:08:08.597 --> 00:08:11.601
<i>But then Varzi had to travel to Berna,</i>


00:08:11.684 --> 00:08:13.520
<i>to Switzerland, and in Berna</i>


00:08:13.603 --> 00:08:16.981
<i>he participated in the Swiss Grand Prix.</i>


00:08:17.064 --> 00:08:19.526
<i>On Thursday afternoon,</i>


00:08:19.609 --> 00:08:24.822
<i>while Varzi was trying out the circuit,</i>


00:08:24.907 --> 00:08:28.869
<i>he had an accident and died.</i>


00:08:31.121 --> 00:08:37.420
<i>When Achille died, I was in Berna,
so I went to his funeral.</i>


00:08:37.503 --> 00:08:41.214
<i>And I hired his mechanic.</i>


00:08:41.299 --> 00:08:45.219
<i>Then Mr. Varzi,
Mr. Menotti Varzi, his father,</i>


00:08:45.304 --> 00:08:48.807
<i>offered me his house for me to live in.</i>


00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:53.395
<i>So I named the Argentinian team
Achille Varzi Argentinian Team,</i>


00:08:53.480 --> 00:08:56.023
<i>to honor Varzi.</i>


00:08:56.857 --> 00:08:59.652
<i>The entire Argentinian team was there.</i>


00:08:59.735 --> 00:09:05.158
<i>There were many Argentinian racers
in the Varzi Villa,</i>


00:09:05.241 --> 00:09:08.454
<i>he had let them stay in his house.</i>


00:09:08.537 --> 00:09:13.917
Then they would go out to the bar at night


00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:18.672
and meet my husband and other people.


00:09:18.755 --> 00:09:21.468
I don't think there's anyone of them left.


00:09:21.551 --> 00:09:24.554
I'm the last one.
The rest of them passed away.


00:09:25.389 --> 00:09:29.226
<i>Every year, he would come in May.</i>


00:09:29.308 --> 00:09:34.313
He would come to Galliate
to visit his old friends.


00:09:37.485 --> 00:09:41.572
<i>Well, I have so many memories.</i>


00:09:42.407 --> 00:09:46.660
Some are good, some are bad.


00:09:46.744 --> 00:09:52.500
But the good ones compensate
for the bad ones.


00:09:52.583 --> 00:09:58.464
Except for an accident I had,
my first accident, in Peru,


00:09:58.549 --> 00:10:02.051
where my co-driver,
which was my friend, died.


00:10:03.052 --> 00:10:06.598
1948 - GRAND PRIX OF SOUTH AMERICA
ROAD RACING


00:10:07.433 --> 00:10:09.809
<i>In the premises
of the Argentine Automobile Club,</i>


00:10:09.893 --> 00:10:13.230
<i>we'll hold the raffle
for the Grand Prix of South America.</i>


00:10:13.313 --> 00:10:16.276
<i>Veteran Ernesto Blanco
draws ball number one,</i>


00:10:16.358 --> 00:10:18.528
<i>with Fangio holding the first position,</i>


00:10:18.611 --> 00:10:20.946
<i>and the second,
his friend, Domingo Marimon.</i>


00:10:21.113 --> 00:10:24.491
<i>From Buenos Aires to Caracas,
the drivers will join efforts</i>


00:10:24.576 --> 00:10:28.914
<i>with the most diverse topographies
in the most difficult race in the world.</i>


00:10:31.750 --> 00:10:34.711
<i>We had several problems.</i>


00:10:34.795 --> 00:10:36.630
<i>There were these regulations that said</i>


00:10:36.713 --> 00:10:40.176
<i>that the starting positions
in each stage of the race</i>


00:10:40.258 --> 00:10:42.969
<i>would vary depending
on the general classification.</i>


00:10:43.054 --> 00:10:47.099
<i>I had some issues in the first stage.</i>


00:10:47.184 --> 00:10:52.104
<i>The pinion and crown gear were worn away,
and that cost me a lot of time.</i>


00:10:52.189 --> 00:10:55.399
<i>I was one of the last ones to reach Salta.</i>


00:10:55.567 --> 00:11:00.947
The next stage was Salta to La Quiaca,
in the border with Bolivia.


00:11:01.030 --> 00:11:05.828
In that stage, I made third place.
I passed 60 cars on the road.


00:11:05.911 --> 00:11:09.957
<i>But in the general classification,
I was in 50-something,</i>


00:11:10.040 --> 00:11:12.918
<i>so the next day,
I had to start from the back again.</i>


00:11:13.001 --> 00:11:15.755
<i>I had to drive on dirt roads
and pass the other cars,</i>


00:11:15.839 --> 00:11:22.304
<i>and that was hard on the car, because
every day I'd break something different.</i>


00:11:22.387 --> 00:11:24.516
<i>And that's how we reached Lima.</i>


00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:27.768
We were about to go to bed
when we were summoned


00:11:27.852 --> 00:11:31.063
to tell us that we had to start the race
that same night, at 10:00 p.m.,


00:11:31.188 --> 00:11:33.108
because a revolutionary attack
was imminent,


00:11:33.190 --> 00:11:35.110
<i>they feared something would happen,</i>


00:11:35.192 --> 00:11:38.696
<i>so they sent us out there without knowing
the circuit, with no sleep at all.</i>


00:11:44.410 --> 00:11:47.789
<i>In Lima, I was in the lead</i>


00:11:47.873 --> 00:11:51.709
<i>when I took a curve faster than I should,</i>


00:11:51.793 --> 00:11:54.796
<i>so I... I didn't faint,</i>


00:11:54.880 --> 00:11:59.553
<i>so while the car was spinning and falling,
I felt like it was a huge disaster,</i>


00:11:59.636 --> 00:12:03.640
<i>as if we were falling off a cliff,
especially because my co-driver</i>


00:12:03.722 --> 00:12:07.144
<i>was thrown off the car
along with the car door.</i>


00:12:13.065 --> 00:12:17.780
<i>That was my first accident.</i>


00:12:17.863 --> 00:12:21.199
<i>I thought I would never race again.</i>


00:12:23.201 --> 00:12:28.206
<i>Urrutia's death...
I caused that accident.</i>


00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:34.463
Fangio, there's a lot of talk
about your health.


00:12:34.548 --> 00:12:38.552
In order to bring some peace of mind
to all car racing fans,


00:12:38.634 --> 00:12:40.554
could you tell us how you feel?


00:12:40.636 --> 00:12:42.723
I'm perfectly well.


00:12:42.806 --> 00:12:47.226
I'm just waiting for my chance
to face new and brave opponents.


00:12:47.311 --> 00:12:49.855
And whether I win or lose,
I'll dedicate all my efforts


00:12:49.939 --> 00:12:53.859
to my good friend Urrutia,
my brother through thick and thin.


00:12:53.943 --> 00:12:56.486
I speak for all of us here
when I say thank you very much.


00:13:00.490 --> 00:13:05.245
<i>There's an old saying that goes,
"There's no substitute for experience."</i>


00:13:05.330 --> 00:13:10.501
I was almost 37
when I started racing in Europe,


00:13:10.586 --> 00:13:14.965
and I kept doing it until I was
an old man, 47 years old.


00:14:12.402 --> 00:14:16.990
SHEFFIELD UNIVERSITY
SHEFFIELD, ENGLAND


00:14:17.073 --> 00:14:22.538
STUDY ON WHO WAS
THE BEST RACER OF ALL TIMES


00:15:52.966 --> 00:15:59.722
<i>When you compare
the cars from the 50s with today's cars,</i>


00:15:59.807 --> 00:16:02.684
<i>I'm talking about competition cars,</i>


00:16:02.769 --> 00:16:05.021
<i>the first point is the comfort.</i>


00:16:05.104 --> 00:16:07.107
<i>These cars weren't comfortable.</i>


00:16:07.190 --> 00:16:09.859
<i>It sounds obvious, right?</i>


00:16:09.944 --> 00:16:15.533
But back then, they were
more interested in having a fast car.


00:16:23.792 --> 00:16:28.087
<i>The gearboxes were difficult to maneuver,
the clutch was stiff, um...</i>


00:16:28.171 --> 00:16:34.385
<i>You had to be an expert
to drive a race car in the 50s.</i>


00:17:00.330 --> 00:17:06.336
<i>Today, the cars are built
with carbon fiber</i>


00:17:06.419 --> 00:17:10.049
<i>and composite materials,
and they're heavily tested.</i>


00:17:10.132 --> 00:17:14.012
<i>The category is much safer,
and the tracks are also safer,</i>


00:17:14.095 --> 00:17:17.223
and that's something
that's definitely changed.


00:17:23.980 --> 00:17:28.360
<i>If you want to be efficient
in today's cars, you need precision.</i>


00:17:29.194 --> 00:17:33.406
<i>If you wanted to be efficient
in the cars from the 50s, you needed art.</i>


00:17:33.573 --> 00:17:36.326
<i>Now, like I always say,</i>


00:17:36.409 --> 00:17:41.789
<i>if you add precision
to the art from the 50s,</i>


00:17:41.874 --> 00:17:43.375
<i>the result is a world champion.</i>


00:17:43.458 --> 00:17:46.795
<i>And if you add art to today's precision,</i>


00:17:46.880 --> 00:17:48.589
<i>the result is also a world champion.</i>


00:17:48.672 --> 00:17:54.428
<i>So what we need to do
is to add to each period</i>


00:17:54.513 --> 00:17:55.846
<i>what that period was missing.</i>


00:18:09.236 --> 00:18:12.073
<i>One of the things
that was certainly helpful</i>


00:18:12.156 --> 00:18:16.201
was my knowledge of mechanics,


00:18:16.285 --> 00:18:21.707
knowing how to properly use a car.


00:18:22.708 --> 00:18:25.628
When I started to race with them,
I'd ask the mechanic


00:18:25.711 --> 00:18:27.838
what's the maximum power for the cars,


00:18:27.923 --> 00:18:31.385
and I never exceeded that maximum.


00:18:33.096 --> 00:18:37.101
MODENA, ITALY


00:18:38.102 --> 00:18:44.108
<i>Today's pilots aren't familiar
with the technical aspects of racing,</i>


00:18:44.191 --> 00:18:48.696
<i>like the pilots in Fangio's time were.</i>


00:18:48.778 --> 00:18:53.075
These days,
the car is driven by the pilot,


00:18:53.158 --> 00:18:56.537
but everything else
is handled in the boxes


00:18:56.620 --> 00:19:01.292
<i>by dozens of engineers
that control the car remotely</i>


00:19:01.375 --> 00:19:06.755
<i>and tell the driver, "Do this or that,"
and the driver does it.</i>


00:19:08.299 --> 00:19:14.724
<i>That's why I've always appreciated
the work of the mechanics.</i>


00:19:14.806 --> 00:19:17.809
<i>They're the real heroes of the races.</i>


00:19:17.892 --> 00:19:22.647
<i>You always mention the pilots,
but never the mechanics.</i>


00:19:22.732 --> 00:19:26.235
<i>Without them, you can't win a race.</i>


00:19:26.318 --> 00:19:30.948
They're the ones that handle
those tiny little details


00:19:31.033 --> 00:19:32.492
that can make you lose a race.


00:19:34.620 --> 00:19:38.706
BRACKLEY, ENGLAND


00:20:11.949 --> 00:20:15.870
1949 - SAN REMO GRAND PRIX, ITALY
OSPEDALETTI CIRCUIT


00:20:31.304 --> 00:20:33.514
<i>In 1949</i>


00:20:33.597 --> 00:20:37.017
<i>he began his international campaign
in the San Remo circuit.</i>


00:20:37.102 --> 00:20:42.441
<i>He started having problems
with his Maserati, mechanical problems,</i>


00:20:42.524 --> 00:20:46.152
<i>and his team leader, Amadeo Vi?ani,</i>


00:20:46.237 --> 00:20:48.488
advised him not to race.


00:20:49.406 --> 00:20:52.909
They were used to work
under any kind of conditions,


00:20:52.992 --> 00:20:57.040
something they brought with them
from their road racing days,


00:20:57.125 --> 00:21:00.544
so he decided to dismantle the engine


00:21:00.627 --> 00:21:04.465
<i>and stayed up all night until 1:00 a.m.
polishing the crankshaft</i>


00:21:04.548 --> 00:21:08.885
<i>to give the bearings a proper alignment
and make everything perfect</i>


00:21:08.970 --> 00:21:12.931
<i>so that, once that work was done,
the mechanics would be able</i>


00:21:13.014 --> 00:21:16.811
<i>to put the engine back together
while he got some sleep</i>


00:21:16.894 --> 00:21:18.856
<i>before the start of the race.</i>


00:21:18.938 --> 00:21:21.275
<i>That was his first race in Europe.</i>


00:21:22.650 --> 00:21:27.071
<i>Welcome to the Ospedaletti circuit,
home of the San Remo Grand Prix.</i>


00:21:27.156 --> 00:21:30.658
<i>Argentinian Juan Manuel Fangio,
in his European debut,</i>


00:21:30.743 --> 00:21:35.539
<i>has won first place in the table,
outdoing his teammate Prince Bira.</i>


00:21:35.623 --> 00:21:38.626
<i>Looks like there's trouble
with Righetti's Ferrari.</i>


00:21:38.709 --> 00:21:42.297
<i>Meanwhile, Fangio takes the lead
at the Ponticello curve.</i>


00:21:46.717 --> 00:21:51.015
<i>Perfect job by Fangio,
winning both heats of the race,</i>


00:21:51.097 --> 00:21:55.019
<i>showing the world
that the new Argentinian Formula 1 team</i>


00:21:55.101 --> 00:21:58.730
<i>is here to fight for the top.</i>


00:21:59.689 --> 00:22:03.109
<i>I was lucky to win that time.</i>


00:22:03.194 --> 00:22:08.866
<i>But you have to take those compliments
with a grain of salt,</i>


00:22:08.949 --> 00:22:12.953
<i>otherwise you might end up believing
that you're the best.</i>


00:22:13.789 --> 00:22:17.209
Trying to be the best in everything?
I agree with that.


00:22:17.292 --> 00:22:19.377
But never believe you're the best.


00:22:21.128 --> 00:22:24.217
MODENA, ITALY


00:22:24.300 --> 00:22:29.012
<i>My name is Adolfo Orsi,
I was born in 1951.</i>


00:22:29.180 --> 00:22:35.186
My family bought Maserati in 1937.


00:22:35.268 --> 00:22:39.898
It stayed in the family until 1969.


00:22:40.732 --> 00:22:47.699
<i>Maserati has been one
of the most important teams</i>


00:22:47.782 --> 00:22:51.285
<i>in Formula 1 during the 1950s,</i>


00:22:51.370 --> 00:22:56.834
<i>because it developed the 250F
not only for the official team,</i>


00:22:56.916 --> 00:22:59.254
<i>but also for the private racers</i>


00:22:59.337 --> 00:23:01.880
that, in those times,
made up a significant part


00:23:01.965 --> 00:23:04.843
of the Formula 1 starting grid.


00:23:06.885 --> 00:23:08.721
<i>My name is Ermmano Cozza.</i>


00:23:08.804 --> 00:23:13.016
<i>I was born in Modena in 1933.</i>


00:23:13.101 --> 00:23:18.565
<i>When I finished technical school,
my love for engines drove me to Maserati.</i>


00:23:19.400 --> 00:23:22.152
<i>I would say that, for Maserati, Fangio,</i>


00:23:22.237 --> 00:23:26.574
<i>in the entire history of Maserati,</i>


00:23:26.657 --> 00:23:31.579
was its most important pilot


00:23:31.662 --> 00:23:32.998
and, in my opinion, the best.


00:23:33.832 --> 00:23:36.543
And he was the best not only for Maserati;


00:23:36.627 --> 00:23:42.008
for Mercedes in 1954/55,
he was also the best.


00:23:45.511 --> 00:23:49.891
<i>Maserati was a family business,</i>


00:23:49.973 --> 00:23:51.726
<i>it was run by the Orsi family,</i>


00:23:51.809 --> 00:23:57.356
<i>and I was good friends with
their chief mechanic, Guerino Bertocchi,</i>


00:23:57.441 --> 00:23:59.318
<i>and with his brother.</i>


00:23:59.401 --> 00:24:01.694
<i>I was very comfortable with them,</i>


00:24:01.778 --> 00:24:06.033
I even raced for them
without signing any contracts.


00:24:06.115 --> 00:24:08.912
And the car ran wonderfully.


00:24:09.745 --> 00:24:13.707
<i>I was fortunate to win 6 races in Europe:</i>


00:24:14.542 --> 00:24:19.212
<i>San Remo, Pau, Perpignan,</i>


00:24:19.297 --> 00:24:23.176
<i>Marseille, Albi.</i>


00:24:25.178 --> 00:24:26.680
<i>The Pau Grand Prix begins.</i>


00:24:26.763 --> 00:24:30.099
<i>Argentinian Fangio and Graffenried
are head to head.</i>


00:24:35.647 --> 00:24:40.361
<i>Argentinian Juan Manuel Fangio
has won three races this year alone.</i>


00:24:40.444 --> 00:24:42.196
<i>Welcome to Marseille.</i>


00:24:42.278 --> 00:24:45.200
<i>Fangio is making
every racer's dream come true,</i>


00:24:45.282 --> 00:24:48.285
<i>winning over his Italian teammates
in their own home</i>


00:24:48.370 --> 00:24:52.707
<i>in the Mecca of car racing,
Monza's historical circuit.</i>


00:24:52.790 --> 00:24:58.295
<i>Racing in Monza was, for any pilot
that was starting racing in Europe,</i>


00:24:58.380 --> 00:25:00.172
<i>a dream come true.</i>


00:25:00.255 --> 00:25:02.385
<i>Fangio is now in the home stretch.</i>


00:25:02.468 --> 00:25:06.472
<i>There's the checkered flag,
and Fangio wins his sixth race this year.</i>


00:25:06.554 --> 00:25:09.140
<i>The Argentinian won!
The victory goes to Fangio!</i>


00:25:09.225 --> 00:25:15.063
From the years 49 to 50,


00:25:15.146 --> 00:25:18.192
Alfa Romeo returns to the races.


00:25:19.026 --> 00:25:21.153
<i>They invited me to a race.</i>


00:25:21.238 --> 00:25:24.533
<i>Mr. Alessio, the president of Alfa Romeo
back in those days,</i>


00:25:24.616 --> 00:25:27.785
<i>had seen me won the previous year
in San Remo.</i>


00:25:29.371 --> 00:25:33.666
<i>The return of Alfa Romeo
was a highly publicized event.</i>


00:25:33.750 --> 00:25:38.213
ALFA ROMEO MUSEUM
ARESE, ITALY


00:25:42.050 --> 00:25:43.886
<i>When World War II ended,</i>


00:25:43.969 --> 00:25:46.889
<i>Alfa Romeo began
a period of reconstruction.</i>


00:25:46.972 --> 00:25:49.308
<i>Their buildings
had been destroyed by bombings.</i>


00:25:49.393 --> 00:25:52.021
<i>Italy in its entirety needed
to be rebuilt,</i>


00:25:52.103 --> 00:25:54.899
<i>so they didn't have
an automobile market.</i>


00:25:54.982 --> 00:25:58.318
<i>There was a pause, but in 1950,</i>


00:25:58.485 --> 00:26:01.071
when the first Formula 1
world championship started,


00:26:01.156 --> 00:26:02.489
a new world was born.


00:26:02.574 --> 00:26:04.324
And Alfa Romeo shows up


00:26:04.409 --> 00:26:07.579
with a perfectly fine-tuned
and evolved car,


00:26:07.662 --> 00:26:11.417
and with a team of champions,
the so-called three Fs team.


00:26:11.499 --> 00:26:16.547
<i>There was Farina, the last Italian
champion from the pre-war era,</i>


00:26:16.629 --> 00:26:19.550
<i>Luigi Fagioli,
who had the most experience,</i>


00:26:19.632 --> 00:26:25.306
<i>and a young Fangio that showed us all
that he's one of the best.</i>


00:26:27.058 --> 00:26:30.604
1950 - SAN REMO GRAND PRIX, ITALY
OSPEDALETTI CIRCUIT


00:26:30.687 --> 00:26:33.565
<i>In San Remo, we had two cars in the race.</i>


00:26:33.647 --> 00:26:38.152
<i>Before it began, Farina hurt his arm,</i>


00:26:38.235 --> 00:26:43.115
<i>so the team decided
to withdraw from the race</i>


00:26:43.200 --> 00:26:47.662
<i>because all they had left was
some unknown racer.</i>


00:26:47.746 --> 00:26:52.542
<i>The people that had put the money
for the publicity complained about this,</i>


00:26:52.627 --> 00:26:54.002
<i>and they started arguing.</i>


00:26:54.837 --> 00:26:58.799
<i>So I intervened, I said,
"Gentlemen, what's the problem here?</i>


00:26:58.882 --> 00:27:03.722
<i>If I lose, only some unknown racer
will lose, not Alfa Romeo.</i>


00:27:03.805 --> 00:27:08.352
Now, if I win, Alfa Romeo wins,
no matter who's driving."


00:27:09.478 --> 00:27:11.771
<i>The San Remo race has started.</i>


00:27:11.855 --> 00:27:15.109
<i>One of the Alfa Romeos
seems to have skidded at the start.</i>


00:27:15.191 --> 00:27:19.530
<i>It's the Argentinian rookie Fangio,
who's losing several positions.</i>


00:27:19.613 --> 00:27:21.824
<i>It's lap number 32,</i>


00:27:21.907 --> 00:27:25.662
<i>Ascari went off the course
and withdrew his car.</i>


00:27:25.745 --> 00:27:28.205
<i>The Argentinian Fangio
makes a stunning come back</i>


00:27:28.288 --> 00:27:31.501
<i>and Froilan Gonzalez and his Maserati
abandon the race.</i>


00:27:31.583 --> 00:27:36.881
<i>Fangio leaves his compatriot Pian
behind with a spectacular maneuver.</i>


00:27:38.049 --> 00:27:40.136
<i>And Fangio does it.</i>


00:27:40.218 --> 00:27:42.804
<i>The checkered flag falls
in front of the Alfetta.</i>


00:27:44.055 --> 00:27:46.474
<i>I was fortunate to win that race.</i>


00:27:46.559 --> 00:27:48.769
<i>The only Alfa in the race.</i>


00:27:55.527 --> 00:28:00.364
<i>That night, we had a meeting
to discuss the contract.</i>


00:28:00.447 --> 00:28:03.285
<i>I told them,
"You fill it and I'll sign it."</i>


00:28:03.367 --> 00:28:09.248
<i>I had a chance to race with the best car
in existence at that time.</i>


00:28:09.331 --> 00:28:11.458
<i>And I didn't have to pay money
or anything,</i>


00:28:11.543 --> 00:28:13.337
<i>I just put my body to their service.</i>


00:28:13.422 --> 00:28:19.428
<i>I think that helped build
a strong friendship</i>


00:28:19.510 --> 00:28:21.262
<i>with the Alfa Romeo team.</i>


00:29:51.275 --> 00:29:55.194
1950 - BRITISH GRAND PRIX
SILVERSTONE CIRCUIT


00:30:11.503 --> 00:30:16.719
<i>Before the start, we're a little nervous,
until we sit inside the car.</i>


00:30:16.802 --> 00:30:19.220
<i>Once you're in the car,</i>


00:30:19.305 --> 00:30:20.848
everything else disappears.


00:30:20.931 --> 00:30:23.474
You become somebody else.


00:30:33.777 --> 00:30:38.116
<i>In Fangio's first racing season in 1950,</i>


00:30:38.200 --> 00:30:40.745
<i>the Alfetta wins 11 out of 11.</i>


00:30:40.828 --> 00:30:44.707
<i>Despite this superiority
and, in a certain way,</i>


00:30:44.789 --> 00:30:47.460
<i>the rivalry between the three teammates,</i>


00:30:47.542 --> 00:30:52.716
<i>their relationship inside the team
was always very positive.</i>


00:30:52.798 --> 00:30:56.635
In some races, the drivers
would make a close formation


00:30:56.720 --> 00:30:58.512
to wait for their teammate to catch up,


00:30:58.597 --> 00:31:01.099
or they would slow down
to warn them about some danger.


00:31:01.182 --> 00:31:05.228
<i>That was a sign that the camaraderie
in those teams was wonderful,</i>


00:31:05.311 --> 00:31:08.523
<i>and that was a time when,
at almost 300 km/h,</i>


00:31:08.608 --> 00:31:10.650
<i>they were risking their lives
in the tracks.</i>


00:31:20.035 --> 00:31:24.540
<i>Back in those days, we had to be careful
not to overwork the cars.</i>


00:31:24.625 --> 00:31:30.046
<i>Otherwise, we would overheat
and exert the engine,</i>


00:31:30.130 --> 00:31:34.550
<i>so as a pilot, it was very useful to me</i>


00:31:34.635 --> 00:31:37.595
<i>to be familiar
with the mechanics of the car.</i>


00:31:45.939 --> 00:31:50.442
STUTTGART, GERMANY


00:31:50.527 --> 00:31:54.196
<i>Fangio had the special ability</i>


00:31:54.280 --> 00:31:58.700
<i>of being prepared</i>


00:31:58.785 --> 00:32:02.497
both physically and emotionally,


00:32:02.580 --> 00:32:05.543
and also mechanically,


00:32:05.625 --> 00:32:10.255
he knew the engine, the clutch,
the brakes, everything about his cars.


00:32:10.338 --> 00:32:14.259
<i>The mechanics used to say that,</i>


00:32:14.342 --> 00:32:18.014
<i>had he taken 3 or 4 more laps,</i>


00:32:18.097 --> 00:32:20.434
<i>the engine would've died.</i>


00:32:20.516 --> 00:32:25.146
He felt like he was making
the most out of himself


00:32:25.229 --> 00:32:27.106
and of the machine


00:32:27.190 --> 00:32:31.069
in order to reach their peak.


00:34:32.030 --> 00:34:36.160
<i>Despite the heavy rain,
Fangio wins this difficult race</i>


00:34:36.244 --> 00:34:39.247
<i>and takes the first place
in the world championship</i>


00:34:39.331 --> 00:34:41.416
<i>with 9 points in his favor.</i>


00:34:49.091 --> 00:34:53.052
1950 - MONACO GRAND PRIX
MONTECARLO CIRCUIT


00:34:55.431 --> 00:34:57.683
<i>Montecarlo was very interesting.</i>


00:34:58.934 --> 00:35:00.936
<i>The day before the race,</i>


00:35:01.021 --> 00:35:06.942
<i>I was at the Montecarlo Automobile Club
looking at some pictures</i>


00:35:07.027 --> 00:35:11.365
<i>when I came across a picture
of an accident from before the war.</i>


00:35:11.448 --> 00:35:17.371
<i>There was this curve called
"The Tabaccaio" that had a tall wall</i>


00:35:17.454 --> 00:35:19.749
<i>and you couldn't see
what was on the other side,</i>


00:35:19.831 --> 00:35:22.877
and in the picture you could see
several cars piled up against it.


00:35:22.959 --> 00:35:25.880
One of the cars spun around
and the ones behind it


00:35:25.962 --> 00:35:27.757
crashed into it.


00:35:27.839 --> 00:35:31.553
<i>I had a very good car,
so when the race started,</i>


00:35:31.638 --> 00:35:34.931
<i>I took the lead and, in the second lap,</i>


00:35:35.016 --> 00:35:38.852
<i>I see a yellow flag waving at us.</i>


00:35:38.935 --> 00:35:42.649
<i>It didn't mean we had to stop.
A yellow flag means precaution.</i>


00:35:42.731 --> 00:35:45.484
<i>Still, that picture came to mind,</i>


00:35:45.567 --> 00:35:51.200
<i>and when I reached the curve,
I saw the cars piled up in a heap.</i>


00:35:51.283 --> 00:35:54.369
<i>So I drove by them very slowly,</i>


00:35:54.452 --> 00:35:58.998
<i>and that gave me the chance to go on,</i>


00:35:59.083 --> 00:36:03.837
<i>because the cars behind me
could do the same thing I did.</i>


00:36:03.922 --> 00:36:07.258
<i>Eight cars were involved in that accident.</i>


00:36:09.678 --> 00:36:14.432
<i>Fangio makes history in Monaco
and wins his first race</i>


00:36:14.515 --> 00:36:17.351
<i>in the top category.</i>


00:36:17.435 --> 00:36:21.691
<i>The Argentinian is now
among the best pilots in the world.</i>


00:36:23.067 --> 00:36:26.903
<i>I was happy because it was
my first world championship.</i>


00:36:26.986 --> 00:36:29.323
<i>I never thought I'd win.</i>


00:36:33.202 --> 00:36:37.248
GENOLIER, SWITZERLAND


00:37:52.328 --> 00:37:57.750
<i>In 1951, Fangio started
his second season with Alfa Romeo.</i>


00:37:57.833 --> 00:38:02.463
<i>He won second place behind Farina
the previous year,</i>


00:38:02.547 --> 00:38:07.719
<i>and this time he needed
to beat team Ferrari</i>


00:38:07.804 --> 00:38:11.640
<i>with his teammate
and friend Froilan Gonzalez.</i>


00:38:11.723 --> 00:38:17.814
<i>Another driver that was very important
in those times was Ascari.</i>


00:38:17.896 --> 00:38:20.482
That was an important year


00:38:20.566 --> 00:38:22.945
because he had made a name for himself,


00:38:23.027 --> 00:38:27.114
but he needed to prove
that he could also win a championship.


00:38:28.075 --> 00:38:31.245
1951 - SPANISH GRAND PRIX, BARCELONA
PEDRALBES CIRCUIT


00:38:55.395 --> 00:39:00.359
<i>In this race,
they had several problems in Alfa.</i>


00:39:00.442 --> 00:39:05.739
<i>They made a car with a de Dion tube
and two lateral tanks,</i>


00:39:05.822 --> 00:39:07.867
<i>to have the other team believe</i>


00:39:07.949 --> 00:39:11.412
<i>that we wouldn't stop
for the entirety of the race,</i>


00:39:11.495 --> 00:39:12.997
<i>which wasn't true.</i>


00:39:29.890 --> 00:39:32.600
<i>That was Ferrari's mistake.</i>


00:39:32.684 --> 00:39:35.228
<i>To match my speed,</i>


00:39:35.396 --> 00:39:40.692
<i>they had to overexert themselves
and the tires would fray,</i>


00:39:40.776 --> 00:39:43.279
<i>so they had to change them
for smaller tires.</i>


00:39:44.195 --> 00:39:48.284
<i>They tried to follow my lead,
and they were left behind.</i>


00:39:48.366 --> 00:39:52.705
<i>I managed to stop, change the tires,
and still maintain the lead position.</i>


00:42:17.943 --> 00:42:20.446
The only two accidents
I had in my entire life


00:42:20.528 --> 00:42:25.033
were due to the same reason:
physical exhaustion.


00:42:25.116 --> 00:42:29.371
If you're tired, you shouldn't drive.


00:42:30.372 --> 00:42:33.876
1952 - ULSTER TOURIST TROPHY
NON-CHAMPIONSHIP CATEGORY


00:42:34.711 --> 00:42:38.881
<i>That Saturday, I raced in Ireland.</i>


00:42:40.049 --> 00:42:46.725
<i>And I had promised I would race
in Monza on Sunday.</i>


00:42:46.808 --> 00:42:52.106
<i>Prince Bira had offered his private jet</i>


00:42:52.188 --> 00:42:53.940
<i>to take me there, to Monza.</i>


00:42:54.023 --> 00:42:58.362
<i>But then Prince Bira's car broke down</i>


00:42:58.444 --> 00:43:01.405
<i>in the middle of a storm,</i>


00:43:01.490 --> 00:43:05.911
<i>so when I finished the race in Ireland,
Prince Bira was already gone.</i>


00:43:05.995 --> 00:43:12.793
<i>So a group of us bought plane tickets
to London, and among us was Rosier.</i>


00:43:12.877 --> 00:43:18.716
<i>In London, I asked if there was some way
to go to Italy from there,</i>


00:43:18.799 --> 00:43:21.803
<i>but there was none, so we went to Paris.</i>


00:43:21.887 --> 00:43:27.393
<i>We arrived in Paris at midnight,
so there were no trains to Italy.</i>


00:43:27.475 --> 00:43:30.603
<i>Rosier lent me his car
to complete the trip.</i>


00:43:36.442 --> 00:43:39.864
<i>Back in those days,
the tunnel didn't exist yet,</i>


00:43:39.947 --> 00:43:43.450
<i>so I had to climb the Moncenisio mountain,</i>


00:43:43.535 --> 00:43:46.913
<i>and arrived in Monza
that Sunday at 2:00 p.m.</i>


00:43:46.996 --> 00:43:49.666
<i>The race was set to start at 2:30 p.m.</i>


00:43:50.667 --> 00:43:53.627
1952 - ITALIAN GRAND PRIX
MONZA CIRCUIT


00:43:53.712 --> 00:43:57.049
<i>The drivers are ready.
In the first line,</i>


00:43:57.132 --> 00:44:01.094
<i>the Ferraris of Farina and Andre Simon.
The current champion, Juan Manuel Fangio,</i>


00:44:01.179 --> 00:44:04.389
<i>will be in the last position
because he didn't participate</i>


00:44:04.472 --> 00:44:06.474
<i>in the classification.</i>


00:44:06.559 --> 00:44:09.437
<i>I started the race from the last position</i>


00:44:09.519 --> 00:44:13.357
<i>and in my second or third lap,</i>


00:44:13.442 --> 00:44:16.945
<i>I was overcoming cars
at a very high speed.</i>


00:44:17.028 --> 00:44:20.616
<i>Fangio, at full speed,
keeps trying to win positions.</i>


00:44:20.699 --> 00:44:23.659
<i>He's on the Lesmo curve,
but his inner side touches the rail.</i>


00:44:23.744 --> 00:44:25.746
<i>The car is out of control.</i>


00:44:32.086 --> 00:44:35.047
<i>When I woke up, I was in the hospital.</i>


00:44:35.130 --> 00:44:37.925
<i>I woke up
maybe three hours after the accident.</i>


00:45:01.992 --> 00:45:04.536
<i>Back in our time, in Formula 1,</i>


00:45:04.621 --> 00:45:08.124
<i>according to the statistics</i>


00:45:08.207 --> 00:45:13.964
<i>by "Auto motor und sport,"</i>


00:45:14.047 --> 00:45:19.719
65 Formula 1 drivers died
during that period.


00:45:19.804 --> 00:45:21.973
65.


00:45:22.056 --> 00:45:25.394
Three, four, five every year.


00:45:25.476 --> 00:45:26.645
Each year.


00:46:15.487 --> 00:46:21.328
<i>I thought it was very easy
to die without realizing it</i>


00:46:21.410 --> 00:46:25.622
<i>and between life and death
there's nothing.</i>


00:46:25.707 --> 00:46:30.879
<i>So when you get back to your senses,</i>


00:46:30.962 --> 00:46:33.256
<i>back to reality,</i>


00:46:33.341 --> 00:46:37.802
you realize that dying is not so terrible,


00:46:37.887 --> 00:46:40.305
you don't have time to think.


00:46:46.354 --> 00:46:50.441
<i>The 1953 season begins,
it's a difficult season,</i>


00:46:50.524 --> 00:46:52.818
<i>it's hard coming back
from an accident,</i>


00:46:52.903 --> 00:46:57.573
<i>going back to the competition rhythm</i>


00:46:57.656 --> 00:47:02.871
<i>and it didn't help that in Argentina,
Holland and Belgium,</i>


00:47:02.953 --> 00:47:07.627
he had problems with his car
and can't finish the competition.


00:47:07.709 --> 00:47:11.171
Then his luck changes.


00:47:11.255 --> 00:47:14.884
<i>He has three second positions
in France, England and Germany.</i>


00:47:14.966 --> 00:47:19.346
<i>And he closes the season
in the most conflictive place: Monza,</i>


00:47:19.429 --> 00:47:22.934
<i>where he had that serious accident.</i>


00:47:23.017 --> 00:47:25.686
<i>He had to prove to himself
and everybody else</i>


00:47:25.771 --> 00:47:30.108
that his ability was intact.


00:47:32.235 --> 00:47:33.946
<i>Autodromo di Monza.</i>


00:47:34.029 --> 00:47:35.864
<i>1953.</i>


00:47:35.949 --> 00:47:39.536
<i>One hundred thousand people came
to the most prestigious race of the year</i>


00:47:39.618 --> 00:47:41.038
<i>the Italian Grand Prix.</i>


00:47:41.121 --> 00:47:43.123
<i>On the starting line, on the front row,</i>


00:47:43.206 --> 00:47:45.835
<i>the ex world champion,
Farina, with number 6,</i>


00:47:45.918 --> 00:47:49.087
<i>Fangio, number 50,
another ex world champion,</i>


00:47:49.171 --> 00:47:52.800
<i>and champion Alberto Ascari,
with number 4.</i>


00:47:54.259 --> 00:47:57.095
ITALIAN GRAND PRIX
MONZA CIRCUIT


00:47:58.889 --> 00:48:01.685
<i>I first met Fangio in Monza</i>


00:48:01.767 --> 00:48:05.396
<i>in September 1953 at the Grand Prix.</i>


00:48:05.856 --> 00:48:09.568
<i>I was in the boxes and...</i>


00:48:09.650 --> 00:48:14.990
<i>it was Fangio, Marimon,
who was Fangio's young apprentice,</i>


00:48:15.073 --> 00:48:16.908
<i>Menditeguy and Mieres.</i>


00:48:16.993 --> 00:48:20.120
And for many laps
they kept going past each other.


00:48:20.204 --> 00:48:24.040
In one lap, Fangio was ahead,
another lap Ascari was ahead.


00:48:24.124 --> 00:48:27.127
In the last lap, Ascari was ahead,


00:48:27.211 --> 00:48:30.882
but he took a bend badly
because Fangio was pressuring him.


00:48:30.965 --> 00:48:33.801
He got off track,
Fangio went past him and won.


00:48:36.597 --> 00:48:39.725
This bend decided the race.


00:48:39.807 --> 00:48:41.226
And I thought,


00:48:41.309 --> 00:48:44.187
"If I manage to keep close
instead of going wide,


00:48:44.270 --> 00:48:46.314
maybe I won't get past,


00:48:46.439 --> 00:48:48.859
my car is stronger when going straight.


00:48:49.026 --> 00:48:50.526
I'll do it in the last lap,


00:48:50.611 --> 00:48:52.821
it's less risky and least expected.


00:48:52.906 --> 00:48:56.117
I tried, I did it, and I won.


00:48:56.200 --> 00:48:57.826
<i>Going for the last lap, Ascari,</i>


00:48:57.911 --> 00:49:01.413
<i>followed by Fangio,
who looks stronger for the straight.</i>


00:49:01.498 --> 00:49:04.125
<i>Fangio went past Ascari.
He's going to beat him.</i>


00:49:04.208 --> 00:49:07.836
<i>Fangio is coming in first.
The speed, the power!</i>


00:49:07.921 --> 00:49:10.508
<i>Fangio won, the champion is back!</i>


00:49:24.522 --> 00:49:27.526
WORLD DRIVERS' CHAMPIONS


00:49:30.319 --> 00:49:33.156
<i>I got along with everyone.</i>


00:49:34.114 --> 00:49:39.203
I never had a rivalry,


00:49:39.287 --> 00:49:42.040
a personal rivalry with anyone.


00:49:42.122 --> 00:49:47.003
The only rivalry was during the race,
not outside the race.


00:49:52.884 --> 00:49:57.932
In the year 1954,


00:49:58.683 --> 00:50:02.814
Mercedes Benz came back


00:50:02.896 --> 00:50:04.606
in the history of that race


00:50:04.691 --> 00:50:08.777
with the famous manager Neubauer.


00:50:09.278 --> 00:50:13.407
And for Neubauer,
there were two drivers:


00:50:13.490 --> 00:50:16.660
one was obviously the best driver,


00:50:16.743 --> 00:50:18.496
Juan Manuel Fangio,


00:50:18.581 --> 00:50:20.708
and Karl Kling.


00:50:20.790 --> 00:50:23.586
Those two had established themselves.


00:50:23.668 --> 00:50:24.712
And me.


00:51:18.185 --> 00:51:22.313
MERCEDES BENZ MUSEUM
STUTTGART, GERMANY


00:51:53.555 --> 00:51:56.558
FRENCH GRAND PRIX
REIMS CIRCUIT


00:52:08.697 --> 00:52:14.077
<i>With Mercedes we did things differently.</i>


00:52:14.993 --> 00:52:21.085
There were three mechanics
for one driver.


00:52:21.167 --> 00:52:24.546
They were exclusive,
not interchangeable.


00:52:24.629 --> 00:52:28.844
<i>Three for me, three for Kling,
three for Hermann.</i>


00:52:28.926 --> 00:52:33.013
<i>We were a force unit.</i>


00:53:08.177 --> 00:53:12.681
<i>I wrote a book together with Carozzo</i>


00:53:12.766 --> 00:53:18.396
<i>and I dedicated a whole chapter
to luck or fate.</i>


00:53:19.980 --> 00:53:24.402
Undoubtedly, you have to help fate,
fate can do it on its own.


00:53:24.485 --> 00:53:27.112
If you sit back, it won't come to you.


00:53:27.197 --> 00:53:31.285
But I do believe we all have


00:53:31.367 --> 00:53:33.703
a delineated path.


00:53:35.164 --> 00:53:38.124
GERMAN GRAND PRIX
NURBURGRING CIRCUIT


00:53:40.877 --> 00:53:44.799
<i>Onofre Marimon, son of Domingo Marimon,
Fangio's great friend,</i>


00:53:44.881 --> 00:53:48.345
<i>was his apprentice
in the European tracks.</i>


00:53:48.428 --> 00:53:51.431
And in Nurburgring circuit,


00:53:51.514 --> 00:53:53.558
an extremely difficult circuit


00:53:53.641 --> 00:53:57.354
because of the elevations,
the amount of bends, the length,


00:53:57.437 --> 00:54:01.982
Juan asked Onofre to wait for him


00:54:02.067 --> 00:54:04.736
to check out some useful details.


00:54:04.821 --> 00:54:07.989
He was an obsessive man,
he'd go to the circuit


00:54:08.074 --> 00:54:09.826
two, three days before.


00:54:09.908 --> 00:54:13.662
If it was a three, four-mile circuit,
he'd do it on his bike.


00:54:13.787 --> 00:54:17.083
If it was 7, 9 miles, like Spa,


00:54:17.165 --> 00:54:20.001
or 14 like Nurburgring,
he'd do it on his motorbike.


00:54:20.086 --> 00:54:24.216
<i>He wanted to memorize the track,</i>


00:54:24.298 --> 00:54:27.219
<i>where he'd place his car,</i>


00:54:27.301 --> 00:54:30.429
<i>what the race-deciding spot was.</i>


00:54:30.513 --> 00:54:34.308
He'd study it days before.


00:54:34.393 --> 00:54:37.604
And he expected his colleague
to do the same.


00:54:40.358 --> 00:54:43.278
<i>Unfortunately,
when Fangio got to the circuit,</i>


00:54:43.361 --> 00:54:46.196
<i>Onofre was already in his car</i>


00:54:46.281 --> 00:54:48.198
<i>and on a downgrade</i>


00:54:48.283 --> 00:54:50.826
<i>he gets off track and dies.</i>


00:55:22.028 --> 00:55:24.070
<i>Marimon was a young man,</i>


00:55:24.155 --> 00:55:27.450
<i>a cheerful man full of life.</i>


00:55:27.533 --> 00:55:31.037
<i>A very likeable fellow, always smiling.</i>


00:55:31.119 --> 00:55:35.082
<i>He was Fangio and Gonzalez's prot?g?.</i>


00:55:35.167 --> 00:55:37.627
<i>They were his mentors.</i>


00:55:38.252 --> 00:55:41.465
I have a photograph, after his death,


00:55:41.548 --> 00:55:45.843
of Fangio and Gonzalez
holding each other in tears.


00:55:45.928 --> 00:55:49.640
For them, it was a real tragedy.


00:55:57.440 --> 00:56:01.151
<i>I believe in predestination.</i>


00:56:01.234 --> 00:56:03.654
No one dies before his time.


00:58:30.936 --> 00:58:33.939
WORLD DRIVERS' CHAMPIONS


00:58:43.198 --> 00:58:45.744
<i>He left for the old continent in 1949</i>


00:58:45.827 --> 00:58:49.455
<i>heading the first international
auto racing Argentine team.</i>


00:58:49.540 --> 00:58:53.334
<i>His past and present success
in the highest auto racing category</i>


00:58:53.417 --> 00:58:54.670
<i>is undeniable.</i>


00:58:54.753 --> 00:58:57.839
<i>Juan Manuel Fangio is
like no other driver.</i>


00:58:57.922 --> 00:59:00.967
<i>He has dominated every circuit.</i>


00:59:01.050 --> 00:59:06.098
<i>Today we can be sure he'll go down
in our country sports history.</i>


00:59:06.181 --> 00:59:08.475
<i>He has achieved
his second championship</i>


00:59:08.560 --> 00:59:10.352
<i>this time with Mercedes Benz.</i>


00:59:11.896 --> 00:59:15.232
<i>Argentineans have gathered
to welcome him this afternoon,</i>


00:59:15.315 --> 00:59:17.777
<i>moved by the Balcarce driver's
achievements</i>


00:59:17.860 --> 00:59:21.657
<i>and excited for the 1955 new season,</i>


00:59:21.740 --> 00:59:24.617
<i>whose first stop
will take place right here.</i>


00:59:27.328 --> 00:59:32.543
<i>Ladies and gentlemen,
welcome to Argentina Grand Prix.</i>


00:59:34.377 --> 00:59:36.756
ARGENTINA GRAND PRIX
BUENOS AIRES CIRCUIT


00:59:36.839 --> 00:59:38.550
<i>All eyes are on Buenos Aires.</i>


00:59:38.632 --> 00:59:42.637
<i>The racing aces will battle
at 17 de Octubre Circuit</i>


00:59:42.722 --> 00:59:44.639
<i>for the third Argentina Grand Prix</i>


00:59:44.724 --> 00:59:47.350
<i>and get points for the World Champions.</i>


00:59:47.435 --> 00:59:49.644
<i>Here's the famous Mercedes Benz team,</i>


00:59:49.729 --> 00:59:52.440
<i>with Fangio, the 1954 world champion.</i>


00:59:54.568 --> 00:59:55.944
<i>In Buenos Aires,</i>


00:59:56.027 --> 01:00:00.406
<i>the team was made up of Fangio, Kling,</i>


01:00:00.489 --> 01:00:02.826
<i>Stirling Moss and me.</i>


01:00:04.911 --> 01:00:08.832
<i>When I first saw Stirling Moss racing,</i>


01:00:08.915 --> 01:00:13.045
<i>I felt great admiration for the guy.</i>


01:00:13.128 --> 01:00:16.506
<i>He was fighting with the best.</i>


01:00:17.342 --> 01:00:22.930
<i>And over the races, we became friends.</i>


01:00:23.013 --> 01:00:27.059
<i>I consider him one of my best friends.</i>


01:00:56.007 --> 01:00:59.426
<i>We had four racing cars.</i>


01:00:59.511 --> 01:01:03.598
<i>And even for Argentina</i>


01:01:03.683 --> 01:01:06.184
<i>the intense hot weather was unusual,</i>


01:01:06.269 --> 01:01:10.480
I think it was in January.


01:01:11.106 --> 01:01:13.191
I started to feel


01:01:13.276 --> 01:01:17.612
I was getting a heatstroke.


01:01:17.697 --> 01:01:21.450
<i>The champion's serenity
can be seen at every bend.</i>


01:01:21.535 --> 01:01:24.871
<i>His strong car and the strong heat
have taken many out.</i>


01:01:24.954 --> 01:01:29.293
<i>It's 151?F where the wheels
touch the track.</i>


01:01:30.459 --> 01:01:33.672
<i>What happened to Moss</i>


01:01:33.754 --> 01:01:39.386
<i>is that he stopped on the track,</i>


01:01:39.469 --> 01:01:43.725
<i>not in the boxes.</i>


01:01:43.808 --> 01:01:46.561
<i>I felt I had to make it
to the boxes at least.</i>


01:01:46.643 --> 01:01:50.772
<i>I got there,
but couldn't get out of the car.</i>


01:01:50.857 --> 01:01:53.735
<i>I was so weak I had to be taken out.</i>


01:01:53.818 --> 01:01:56.612
<i>Karl Kling was thrown of his car.</i>


01:01:56.697 --> 01:01:59.532
<i>Fortunately, nothing happened to him.</i>


01:01:59.615 --> 01:02:03.411
<i>I just remembered
something else that happened.</i>


01:02:03.829 --> 01:02:05.538
<i>During that race,</i>


01:02:06.414 --> 01:02:09.418
<i>he went directly to the boxes</i>


01:02:10.544 --> 01:02:12.254
<i>and, who knows why,</i>


01:02:12.339 --> 01:02:14.925
<i>a bottle of water
was poured on his head,</i>


01:02:15.008 --> 01:02:17.887
<i>and he was put back on the track.</i>


01:02:18.636 --> 01:02:21.681
<i>The great Argentine driver
handles his car marvelously</i>


01:02:21.766 --> 01:02:24.352
<i>displaying admirable physical condition.</i>


01:02:24.434 --> 01:02:28.856
<i>Together with Mieres, he's the only one
racing the full three hours.</i>


01:02:34.488 --> 01:02:39.951
<i>Fangio wins! The great champion
wins Argentina Grand Prix!</i>


01:02:48.460 --> 01:02:52.339
<i>Fangio had to be taken
to the hospital afterwards</i>


01:02:52.422 --> 01:02:56.051
<i>due to serious burns on his legs
because of the hot weather.</i>


01:03:41.766 --> 01:03:45.438
Conditions are different now.


01:03:45.520 --> 01:03:49.942
But just as Fangio raced
with burns on his leg,


01:03:50.025 --> 01:03:52.527
I remember a race at Baren in 2009,


01:03:52.610 --> 01:03:57.616
where a radiator tube came off


01:03:57.701 --> 01:04:00.579
in the direction of the cockpit,
of my seat,


01:04:00.661 --> 01:04:05.374
<i>I got burns on my back
and I got severely dehydrated</i>


01:04:05.459 --> 01:04:08.544
<i>due the burns
and the heat in the cockpit.</i>


01:04:08.627 --> 01:04:12.631
Different levels, different circumstances,


01:04:12.716 --> 01:04:15.427
but in auto racing


01:04:15.510 --> 01:04:19.139
drivers still face extreme conditions.


01:04:19.223 --> 01:04:22.142
But it's safer now, thankfully.


01:04:22.226 --> 01:04:26.646
24 HOURS OF LE MANS, FRANCE
WORLD CHAMPIONSHIP FOR MAKES


01:05:01.726 --> 01:05:06.606
<i>The accident at Le Mans happened
in front of me.</i>


01:05:07.901 --> 01:05:10.986
<i>It was the time for the swap.</i>


01:05:12.113 --> 01:05:15.241
<i>Hawthorn, in the heat of the moment,</i>


01:05:15.324 --> 01:05:18.577
<i>got into the boxes a bit too fast</i>


01:05:18.662 --> 01:05:22.623
<i>and Mackling swerved out from behind,</i>


01:05:23.332 --> 01:05:28.213
<i>at that moment,
Levegh swerved to go past,</i>


01:05:28.297 --> 01:05:32.051
<i>he went over Mackling's wheels,</i>


01:05:32.134 --> 01:05:34.469
<i>killing many people.</i>


01:05:54.575 --> 01:05:58.161
<i>I held tight waiting for the crash,
but the path cleared.</i>


01:05:58.244 --> 01:06:02.083
<i>After I went past,
Mackling's car crashed into the box</i>


01:06:02.166 --> 01:06:04.127
<i>and killed the people in the box.</i>


01:06:08.715 --> 01:06:12.010
<i>Everybody thought
I had caused the accident.</i>


01:06:12.093 --> 01:06:14.803
We were all going very fast.


01:06:14.886 --> 01:06:18.601
Way too fast. Hawthorn too.


01:07:51.532 --> 01:07:55.453
WORLD DRIVERS' CHAMPIONS


01:08:06.005 --> 01:08:08.174
<i>In 1956,</i>


01:08:08.259 --> 01:08:11.928
<i>when Mercedes retired</i>


01:08:12.011 --> 01:08:14.598
<i>from motor racing,</i>


01:08:14.681 --> 01:08:19.562
<i>I knew Ferrari or Maserati
would want me.</i>


01:08:20.521 --> 01:08:25.651
<i>So I talked with Neubauer
and asked for his advice,</i>


01:08:25.736 --> 01:08:30.030
<i>"If Ferrari and Maserati want me,
which one should I pick?"</i>


01:08:30.115 --> 01:08:32.159
<i>"Ferrari," he said.</i>


01:09:19.124 --> 01:09:21.960
FRENCH GRAND PRIX
REIMS CIRCUIT


01:09:44.777 --> 01:09:49.700
<i>When I raced for Ferrari,
some problems cropped up.</i>


01:09:49.783 --> 01:09:52.453
<i>Ferrari's system was different.</i>


01:09:53.371 --> 01:09:56.540
<i>You didn't have an exclusive mechanic.</i>


01:09:56.623 --> 01:10:00.126
<i>Every mechanic work on every car.</i>


01:10:10.389 --> 01:10:14.141
<i>Castellotti got mad at that race.</i>


01:10:14.226 --> 01:10:17.479
<i>He wasn't satisfied with his car.</i>


01:10:17.562 --> 01:10:19.772
He said, "I'm going to quit."


01:10:19.857 --> 01:10:24.528
And I said, "If you're the only one
quitting, nothing will change."


01:10:24.612 --> 01:10:28.782
So, in solidarity with Castellotti,


01:10:28.867 --> 01:10:31.411
who was my friend, I quit, too.


01:10:32.370 --> 01:10:36.374
For any driver
in the history of the sport,


01:10:36.457 --> 01:10:41.170
the goal has always been
racing for Ferrari.


01:10:41.255 --> 01:10:45.134
<i>Ferrari is Formula 1, it's the myth.</i>


01:10:45.216 --> 01:10:47.677
<i>The most incredible thing in Formula 1.</i>


01:10:47.761 --> 01:10:52.724
<i>You watch a Formula 1 race,
and 70%, 80% of the audience</i>


01:10:52.807 --> 01:10:57.022
<i>is wearing the red cap with the horse
on the yellow background.</i>


01:10:57.855 --> 01:11:00.983
Fangio was the only one
who said no to Ferrari.


01:11:02.067 --> 01:11:04.820
<i>Ferrari wouldn't let me go.</i>


01:11:04.905 --> 01:11:08.283
<i>So I set one condition.</i>


01:11:08.365 --> 01:11:11.912
<i>I wanted a mechanic</i>


01:11:11.994 --> 01:11:14.623
<i>exclusively for my car</i>


01:11:14.706 --> 01:11:18.502
<i>because all the mechanics work
on all the cars.</i>


01:11:18.585 --> 01:11:21.422
<i>There wasn't one person in charge.</i>


01:11:21.505 --> 01:11:27.428
<i>Ferrari accepted and gave me
a mechanic in charge of my car.</i>


01:11:27.511 --> 01:11:31.724
<i>And I was able to win
the following races.</i>


01:11:33.477 --> 01:11:36.480
ITALIAN GRAND PRIX
MONZA CIRCUIT


01:11:50.411 --> 01:11:53.457
<i>In the tough hellish Monza circuit,</i>


01:11:53.540 --> 01:11:56.710
<i>the best drivers of the world
will battle for the prize.</i>


01:11:56.793 --> 01:12:00.880
<i>For our compatriot Fangio
his result here will be key.</i>


01:12:00.965 --> 01:12:04.676
<i>A new world title depends on it.</i>


01:12:06.846 --> 01:12:12.267
That day in Monza,
if Fangio came out third or fourth,


01:12:12.352 --> 01:12:16.356
he'd get enough points
to win the championship.


01:12:16.438 --> 01:12:20.527
He even said to his teammates,
Muzzi and Castellotti,


01:12:20.610 --> 01:12:23.614
that they shouldn't worry about the race.


01:12:23.697 --> 01:12:27.283
He wasn't going to fight
for the first positions.


01:12:30.871 --> 01:12:36.793
<i>At one moment in the race,
he began having difficulty holding.</i>


01:12:36.877 --> 01:12:39.713
<i>And he detected a problem
in one of his front wheels.</i>


01:12:39.796 --> 01:12:43.593
<i>They managed to find</i>


01:12:43.676 --> 01:12:46.888
<i>a crack in the steering column.</i>


01:12:47.471 --> 01:12:52.685
They ask Musso for his car
so that Fangio could finish the race


01:12:52.769 --> 01:12:56.148
and get the points
to win the championship.


01:12:56.230 --> 01:12:59.234
<i>Musso refuses to do it,
but moments later,</i>


01:12:59.317 --> 01:13:02.530
<i>Peter Collins,
who saw what was going on,</i>


01:13:02.613 --> 01:13:04.907
<i>got out of his car and gave it to him.</i>


01:13:07.285 --> 01:13:09.703
<i>In the 35th lap, a surprise.</i>


01:13:09.787 --> 01:13:12.540
<i>Collins, with a big gesture,
gives his car to Fangio,</i>


01:13:12.623 --> 01:13:14.917
<i>and El Chueco gets the second position</i>


01:13:15.000 --> 01:13:17.588
<i>behind Stirling Moss,
enough to become</i>


01:13:17.671 --> 01:13:20.591
<i>world champion for the fourth time.</i>


01:13:24.218 --> 01:13:27.722
WORLD DRIVERS' CHAMPIONS


01:13:50.747 --> 01:13:54.210
Fangio was a true gentleman,
a kind, polite man,


01:13:54.292 --> 01:13:56.878
but he was distrustful.


01:13:56.962 --> 01:14:00.548
He suggested
how the car should be built,


01:14:00.633 --> 01:14:02.425
how it should be prepared,


01:14:02.510 --> 01:14:04.761
the right connections.


01:14:04.845 --> 01:14:07.932
He'd drive around the circuit


01:14:08.015 --> 01:14:10.977
and suggest changing
some connections.


01:14:11.060 --> 01:14:15.983
<i>And Ferrari didn't listen
to drivers' instructions, like his.</i>


01:14:16.066 --> 01:14:20.530
<i>There was never a good understanding
between them.</i>


01:14:20.613 --> 01:14:24.783
So when his contract was over,
he came back to Maserati.


01:14:24.908 --> 01:14:27.871
He always said he was
most comfortable with Maserati,


01:14:27.996 --> 01:14:30.749
more than with any other maker.


01:14:40.216 --> 01:14:44.681
<i>In 1957, Maserati debuted the 250F</i>


01:14:44.764 --> 01:14:46.641
<i>with a 12-cylinder engine,</i>


01:14:46.724 --> 01:14:48.809
<i>a more powerful engine</i>


01:14:48.893 --> 01:14:51.436
<i>than the 6-cylinder 250F.</i>


01:14:51.812 --> 01:14:54.566
<i>The 12-cylinder was stronger</i>


01:14:54.649 --> 01:14:56.316
<i>but heavier,</i>


01:14:56.401 --> 01:14:59.486
<i>so it used up a lot of fuel</i>


01:14:59.571 --> 01:15:01.948
<i>and wear out the tires.</i>


01:15:02.032 --> 01:15:08.371
Finally, Fangio said he preferred
driving the 6-cylinder


01:15:08.454 --> 01:15:13.169
and he'll get that half a second
difference himself.


01:15:14.460 --> 01:15:18.215
<i>I wasn't an extraordinary driver.</i>


01:15:18.300 --> 01:15:21.510
<i>I worked within my means</i>


01:15:21.595 --> 01:15:24.388
<i>and calculated my rival's strength.</i>


01:15:24.471 --> 01:15:29.977
At that time, I had to do things
I may have never done.


01:15:30.519 --> 01:15:34.899
GERMAN GRAND PRIX
NURBURGRING CIRCUIT


01:15:40.323 --> 01:15:43.743
<i>The stakes were against me.</i>


01:15:43.826 --> 01:15:48.080
<i>My car wore out the tires
unlike the opposing team's.</i>


01:15:48.163 --> 01:15:52.000
<i>So I said, "Add less fuel,</i>


01:15:52.084 --> 01:15:54.378
<i>I'll be able to go faster."</i>


01:15:54.463 --> 01:15:57.673
<i>And they'd change my tires
halfway into the race.</i>


01:16:10.230 --> 01:16:13.858
<i>When they were off,</i>


01:16:13.941 --> 01:16:18.070
<i>he let Hawthorn and Collins
with Ferrari go past him.</i>


01:16:18.153 --> 01:16:21.198
<i>He let them stay ahead for two laps.</i>


01:16:21.281 --> 01:16:24.910
<i>And they got better times
than their pole times,</i>


01:16:24.994 --> 01:16:27.412
<i>he wanted his tires to get hotter.</i>


01:16:27.498 --> 01:16:31.042
<i>After the second lap,
he started speeding up,</i>


01:16:31.126 --> 01:16:36.422
<i>and improved his times:
9:38, 9:36, 9:34, etc.</i>


01:16:36.507 --> 01:16:39.301
By the time he stopped at the 12nd lap,


01:16:39.386 --> 01:16:43.305
he was 23 seconds ahead.


01:16:43.390 --> 01:16:46.351
If he changed his tires in 22 seconds


01:16:46.434 --> 01:16:49.062
and was back on the race
first or second,


01:16:49.146 --> 01:16:51.774
he'd go past his rivals in the next lap.


01:16:51.857 --> 01:16:54.819
Everything went as planned.


01:16:54.902 --> 01:16:58.698
Fangio got to the boxes
with some advantage.


01:16:58.781 --> 01:17:00.365
<i>He stopped.</i>


01:17:00.448 --> 01:17:03.537
<i>Whiles his tires were being changed,</i>


01:17:03.619 --> 01:17:08.166
<i>one of the wing nuts fell under the car.</i>


01:17:08.249 --> 01:17:11.920
<i>So when the mechanic
had to let Fangio go,</i>


01:17:12.003 --> 01:17:14.255
realized there was missing nut.


01:17:16.090 --> 01:17:20.179
This fell off under the car.


01:17:20.262 --> 01:17:23.640
The chronometer kept going.


01:17:23.725 --> 01:17:28.313
A new nut was brought.


01:17:28.395 --> 01:17:32.232
But instead of 22 seconds,
he lost 58 or 59.


01:17:32.317 --> 01:17:34.776
The race was a lost cause.


01:17:35.445 --> 01:17:38.656
<i>El Chueco zoomed back to get the lead.</i>


01:17:38.741 --> 01:17:42.077
<i>He steps on the gas
and the gap gets shorter.</i>


01:17:42.160 --> 01:17:47.415
<i>He gets closer by the second
and beats his rivals' resistance.</i>


01:17:50.919 --> 01:17:53.421
<i>I started stepping up gears.</i>


01:17:53.504 --> 01:17:55.674
<i>I moved from second into third gear.</i>


01:17:55.759 --> 01:17:58.552
<i>From third to fourth gear.</i>


01:17:58.635 --> 01:18:00.637
<i>And I gained a lot of time.</i>


01:18:00.722 --> 01:18:03.015
<i>I started beating the records.</i>


01:18:03.099 --> 01:18:08.270
<i>In the second-to-last lap,
I got passed Collins.</i>


01:18:08.355 --> 01:18:10.899
<i>Then Collins got passed me.</i>


01:18:10.982 --> 01:18:15.070
<i>And finally I got passed him again
and reached Hawthorn</i>


01:18:15.153 --> 01:18:16.864
<i>at an upward bend.</i>


01:18:16.947 --> 01:18:20.368
When he swerved, I could sneak in.


01:18:20.450 --> 01:18:25.205
And I could take the straight
without the cars suction.


01:18:31.337 --> 01:18:34.215
<i>I won the race by four seconds.</i>


01:18:38.052 --> 01:18:40.013
<i>The nicest thing about it, though,</i>


01:18:40.096 --> 01:18:44.518
<i>was that the two drivers came up
to hug me after the race.</i>


01:18:44.600 --> 01:18:47.104
<i>As if I were their father.</i>


01:18:47.187 --> 01:18:49.899
<i>Hawthorn and Collins, both of them.</i>


01:18:49.982 --> 01:18:55.405
<i>I had won a practically impossible race.</i>


01:18:56.030 --> 01:18:59.033
WORLD DRIVERS' CHAMPIONS


01:20:35.594 --> 01:20:40.016
REIMS, FRANCE


01:20:41.518 --> 01:20:47.231
<i>When you're in your prime,
it's very hard to retire</i>


01:20:47.314 --> 01:20:50.027
<i>in any aspect of life.</i>


01:20:50.110 --> 01:20:54.447
<i>I talked with the mechanic,
I said that if the car worked well</i>


01:20:54.532 --> 01:20:57.242
<i>I'd do four races that year.</i>


01:20:57.325 --> 01:21:01.371
From the start, my car didn't hold well.


01:21:01.454 --> 01:21:06.251
I asked why was that, and they said
they changed the absorbers.


01:21:06.334 --> 01:21:08.672
"Why didn't you put the same?"


01:21:08.754 --> 01:21:11.882
"Because these people pay more."


01:21:11.967 --> 01:21:15.178
It was becoming more commercial.


01:21:15.261 --> 01:21:20.724
<i>And in the 15th lap,
my clutch pedal got broken.</i>


01:21:20.809 --> 01:21:23.018
<i>I had no clutch pedal.</i>


01:21:24.063 --> 01:21:26.065
<i>I strongly believe in fate.</i>


01:21:26.148 --> 01:21:29.987
<i>And a lot had happened that year.</i>


01:21:30.070 --> 01:21:33.489
<i>I'd been to Cuba and was kidnapped
and couldn't race.</i>


01:21:33.574 --> 01:21:36.659
<i>In Indianapolis,
I got a car that didn't work.</i>


01:21:36.742 --> 01:21:39.787
So during a race, I started thinking


01:21:40.455 --> 01:21:44.085
about my parents,
who were quite old by that time.


01:21:45.086 --> 01:21:50.257
<i>I thought, "I still have time to make up
for everything they went through</i>


01:21:50.341 --> 01:21:55.804
<i>while I've been racing around the world,"
because your family suffers.</i>


01:21:55.887 --> 01:21:57.889
<i>So a lot of things were piling up.</i>


01:21:57.974 --> 01:22:02.187
<i>My plan was racing for one year
and I'd been racing for ten.</i>


01:22:02.270 --> 01:22:05.107
<i>I grew tired of racing.</i>


01:22:05.190 --> 01:22:08.818
<i>So during a race, I decided,
"When this is over, I'll retire."</i>


01:22:08.901 --> 01:22:10.362
<i>And so I did.</i>


01:23:23.897 --> 01:23:26.150
<i>Retirement for a sports person</i>


01:23:26.235 --> 01:23:30.405
<i>or anyone with an extremely intense
activity,</i>


01:23:30.488 --> 01:23:33.408
<i>especially one like this,</i>


01:23:33.491 --> 01:23:37.955
<i>is the hardest part of their life.</i>


01:23:38.037 --> 01:23:40.540
<i>Readapting</i>


01:23:40.623 --> 01:23:42.917
is the most difficult part.


01:23:43.000 --> 01:23:45.588
He managed it well.


01:23:45.671 --> 01:23:49.217
<i>He went back to work,</i>


01:23:49.300 --> 01:23:53.471
<i>always related with Mercedes Benz,
first as a car dealer,</i>


01:23:53.554 --> 01:23:58.227
<i>and he kept an active life,</i>


01:23:58.310 --> 01:24:03.108
<i>a life committed
to his past in the sports.</i>


01:24:21.710 --> 01:24:24.421
That car is perfect.


01:25:03.671 --> 01:25:06.382
<i>There's a book in Argentina</i>


01:25:06.465 --> 01:25:10.637
<i>entitled, "When the man is bigger
than the myth."</i>


01:25:10.720 --> 01:25:16.310
<i>I believe that book title</i>


01:25:16.393 --> 01:25:21.816
reflects perfectly well what Fangio is.


01:25:21.898 --> 01:25:25.109
<i>In my last book,</i>


01:25:25.194 --> 01:25:27.904
<i>I mention all the drivers and friends,</i>


01:25:27.987 --> 01:25:30.032
<i>Rodriguez, Linge...</i>


01:25:30.115 --> 01:25:31.326
And here we have


01:25:31.409 --> 01:25:36.957
my very dear friend,
Juan Manuel Fangio.


01:25:37.039 --> 01:25:39.917
Let me read a bit.


01:25:40.000 --> 01:25:42.920
"Fangio was, to me, the driver


01:25:43.003 --> 01:25:45.842
that thanks to his skills


01:25:45.924 --> 01:25:48.885
and despite his humble origins,


01:25:48.970 --> 01:25:53.765
had a radiant presence
whenever he came into a room,


01:25:53.850 --> 01:25:57.478
you could tell he was someone special.


01:25:57.561 --> 01:26:00.106
He was the best."


01:26:28.970 --> 01:26:32.474
<i>When I was a kid I read a book</i>


01:26:32.557 --> 01:26:36.395
<i>in second grade, I think,
and there's a boy asking his granddad,</i>


01:26:36.478 --> 01:26:41.401
<i>"Is it sad to grow old,
have white hair and wrinkles?"</i>


01:26:41.484 --> 01:26:45.780
<i>And his granddad answers,
"Not if you did your duty."</i>


01:26:46.489 --> 01:26:48.408
<i>And that's true.</i>


01:26:48.491 --> 01:26:50.993
<i>You can't be afraid of death.</i>


01:26:51.076 --> 01:26:54.414
<i>Death will come at the right time.
It's only natural.</i>


01:26:54.497 --> 01:26:57.751
<i>If we realized
that one day we'd be gone,</i>


01:26:57.834 --> 01:27:00.421
<i>we would all be nicer.</i>


01:27:00.504 --> 01:27:02.839
<i>That's for sure.</i>


01:28:43.195 --> 01:28:47.743
LORETO FANGIO FAMILY


01:29:17.983 --> 01:29:21.987
JUAN MANUEL FANGIO WON
5 WORLD CHAMPIONSHIPS


01:29:38.506 --> 01:29:41.925
PARTICIPATED IN 51 RACES
GOT 29 POLES


01:29:42.008 --> 01:29:45.053
GOT 23 FAST LAPS
GOT ON 35 PODIUMS


01:29:45.136 --> 01:29:47.848
WON 24 VICTORIES


01:29:58.360 --> 01:30:04.156
ALL THESE PERCENTAGES ARE
THE HIGHEST IN THE CATEGORY

